To calculate the probability of rolling a 4 with a fair six-sided die, we can use the formula:
P(rolling a 4) = 1/6
This is because there is only one way to roll a 4 out of six possible outcomes.
Here's a sample code that uses the Monte Carlo method to estimate the probability of rolling a 4 after 1000 rolls:
```
using System;
class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        int numRolls = 1000;
        int numFours = 0;
        Random rand = new Random();
        for (int i = 0; i < numRolls; i++)
        {
            int roll = rand.Next(1, 7);
            if (roll == 4)
            {
                numFours++;
            }
        }
        double probability = (double)numFours / numRolls;
        Console.WriteLine("Probability of rolling a 4 after {0} rolls: {1}", numRolls, probability);
    }
}
```
In this code, we first set the number of rolls to 1000 and initialize a counter for the number of fours rolled. We then create a new instance of the `Random` class to generate random numbers for each roll.
We then use a `for` loop to simulate the specified number of rolls. For each roll, we generate a random number between 1 and 6 using the `Next` method of the `Random` class. If the roll is a 4, we increment the `numFours` counter.
After all rolls have been simulated, we calculate the probability of rolling a 4 by dividing the number of fours by the total number of rolls. We then output the result to the console.

