To make the page interactive and user-friendly, you can add some JavaScript code to allow users to filter and sort the table based on their preferences. Here's an example of how you can do that:
```
<script>
  // Get the table element
  const table = document.querySelector('table');
  // Get the table rows
  const rows = Array.from(table.querySelectorAll('tr'));
  // Add event listener to the select element
  const select = document.querySelector('select');
  select.addEventListener('change', () => {
    // Get the selected option value
    const value = select.value;
    // Sort the rows based on the selected option
    rows.sort((a, b) => {
      const aText = a.querySelector(`td:nth-child(${value})`).textContent;
      const bText = b.querySelector(`td:nth-child(${value})`).textContent;
      return aText.localeCompare(bText);
    });
    // Append the sorted rows to the table
    rows.forEach(row => table.appendChild(row));
  });
  // Add event listener to the search input
  const input = document.querySelector('input');
  input.addEventListener('input', () => {
    // Get the search query
    const query = input.value.toLowerCase();
    // Filter the rows based on the search query
    rows.forEach(row => {
      const text = row.textContent.toLowerCase();
      if (text.includes(query)) {
        row.style.display = '';
      } else {
        row.style.display = 'none';
      }
    });
  });
</script>
```
This JavaScript code adds two event listeners: one for the select element that allows users to sort the table based on the selected option, and one for the search input that allows users to filter the table based on the search query.

