Here's an example Java code:
import java.util.ArrayList;
public class FoodStorageSystem {
  private ArrayList<Double> temperatureHistory;
  public FoodStorageSystem() {
    temperatureHistory = new ArrayList<>();
  }
  public void addTemperature(double temperature) {
    temperatureHistory.add(temperature);
  }
  public boolean isSpoiled() {
    if (temperatureHistory.size() < 2) {
      return false;
    }
    double currentTemp = temperatureHistory.get(temperatureHistory.size() - 1);
    double previousTemp = temperatureHistory.get(temperatureHistory.size() - 2);
    return currentTemp > 4.44 && currentTemp > previousTemp;
  }
}
This code defines a FoodStorageSystem class that keeps track of temperature readings and determines if the food is spoiled based on the temperature history. The isSpoiled() method returns true if the current temperature is greater than 40 degrees Fahrenheit (4.44 Celsius) and has increased since the previous reading. By regularly checking the temperature and utilizing this code, food spoilage can be prevented.

