For storing and manipulating a matrix data type in C++, one efficient way is to use a two-dimensional array. You can initialize the array with the desired dimensions and fill it with the matrix values. For example:
int matrix[3][3] = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}, {7, 8, 9}};
This creates a 3x3 matrix with the values 1-9. You can perform various operations on the matrix, such as multiplication, by iterating through the rows and columns of the matrix. Here's an example code snippet to multiply two matrices together:
int a[2][2] = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}};
int b[2][2] = {{5, 6}, {7, 8}};
int c[2][2];
for(int i=0; i<2; i++) {
    for(int j=0; j<2; j++) {
        c[i][j] = 0;
        for(int k=0; k<2; k++) {
            c[i][j] += a[i][k] * b[k][j];
        }
