To determine the exact number of petals on a flower, you will need to use some form of computer vision or image processing algorithm to count the number of petals. This can be a complex task, especially if the petals are overlapping or if there are other objects in the image that need to be filtered out.
One possible approach is to use edge detection algorithms to identify the boundaries of the petals, and then count the number of distinct regions within those boundaries. You can also use color segmentation techniques to isolate the pink color of the petals and then count the number of connected components within that color range.
Here is some sample PHP code for edge detection using the Canny algorithm:
```
<?php
// Load the image
$img = imagecreatefromjpeg('flower.jpg');
// Convert to grayscale
imagefilter($img, IMG_FILTER_GRAYSCALE);
// Apply Gaussian blur
imagefilter($img, IMG_FILTER_GAUSSIAN_BLUR);
// Apply Canny edge detection
$edges = imagecreatefromgd(imagefilter($img, IMG_FILTER_EDGEDETECT));
// Count the number of distinct regions within the edges
$regions = array();
for ($x = 0; $x < imagesx($edges); $x++) {
  for ($y = 0; $y < imagesy($edges); $y++) {
    $color = imagecolorat($edges, $x, $y);
    if (!isset($regions[$color])) {
      $regions[$color] = true;
    }
  }
}
$num_petals = count($regions);
// Output the result
echo "The flower has $num_petals petals.";
?>
```
Note that this is just one possible approach, and you may need to experiment with different algorithms and parameters to get the best results for your specific image. Additionally, this code does not take into account the variability in petal sizes, so you may need to combine it with other techniques to accurately count the number of petals.

